  "verbalization": "Dapagliflozin, a sodium-glucose transporter 2 (Sglt2) inhibitor, is linked to Ugt1a9 through a series of indirect relationships. The evidence suggests that Dapagliflozin, through its inclusion in the Sglt2 inhibitor class, binds to Ugt1a9, which is a key enzyme in drug metabolism. This connection is supported by multiple paths that highlight the shared ancestry of Dapagliflozin and Canagliflozin, both of which are Sglt2 inhibitors and share a common lowest common ancestor in the C-Glycosyl Compound category. The observed relationships, including the causation of vulvitis and haematocrit increased by Canagliflozin, further reinforce the notion that Ugt1a9 is involved in the metabolic processing of drugs, particularly those that are glycosylated. The integration of these pathways suggests that Ugt1a9 may play a role in the pharmacokinetics of Dapagliflozin, potentially influencing its efficacy or side effects. The evidence points to a mechanistic link between Dapagliflozin and Ugt1a9, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
